Follow xterm where the two disagreed

Found by sending the same random sequences to xterm and to this library
and comparing every cell and the cursor (BeerSSH's tool/check-xterm.py).
What programs meet, most of it:

- CUU, CUD, CNL and CPL stop at a margin the cursor has not passed, in
  origin mode or not; they ran through it.
- ICH and DCH work on any row; a cursor above the top margin made them
  do nothing.
- Each screen has its own saved cursor, so a full-screen program's DECSC
  no longer moves the cursor its shell comes back to.
- CSI s and CSI u save and restore the cursor without DECLRMM. CSI s
  went home, and took a script's "\e[s" with it.
- REP wraps, scrolls and inserts as printed text does, up to a screenful.
- IL and DL go to the left margin.
- ED 1 at the top left erases the cell.
- An invalid DECSTBM is ignored, not obeyed as a reset and a home, and a
  region is two lines at least.
- DECALN drops the margins and goes home.
- CPR is relative to the margins in origin mode.
- DECRC restores the pending wrap, stays inside the margins in origin
  mode, and with nothing saved goes home after RIS or DECSTR.
- Erasing, inserting, deleting and RI, IND, NEL and LF that scroll cancel
  the pending wrap.
- Mode 47 is the alternate screen.
- DECSTR keeps the tab stops, LNM and the alternate screen.
- A wide glyph with one half overwritten or erased goes whole; at the
  edge the cursor is on its last column; with wrapping off it stays on
  the line.

